The hoovr binary is written in Rust. To agree with lintr and styler it reimplements
parts of them, and some of that is close enough to their code, or copies their data,
that it is credited here. The Rust crates the binary is built with are credited
separately, in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES in each release zip. The R package hoovr, which
downloads and runs the binary, carries this notice too; its own R code contains none of
what is listed below.


lintr (https://github.com/r-lib/lintr), version 3.4.0
Copyright (c) 2014-2025 lintr authors. MIT License, text below.

  - Ported from lintr's linters, step for step over R's parse-data tree: the default
    configuration of indentation_linter; three of brace_linter's checks;
    return_linter's implicit style; spaces_inside_linter and
    spaces_left_parentheses_linter; object_name_linter's object_name_xpath; and
    implicit_assignment_linter's implicit_assignment_xpath.
  - Copied from lintr: the special_funs list, and the .base_s3_generics table of the
    generics whose methods object_name_linter and object_length_linter exempt.


styler (https://github.com/r-lib/styler), version 1.11.0
Copyright (c) 2021 styler authors. MIT License, text below.

  - Ported from styler: token_is_on_aligned_line(), the test for whether code is
    aligned in columns, which the formatter uses to keep that alignment.


rex (https://github.com/r-lib/rex), version 1.2.2
Copyright (c) 2014-2016 James Hester and Kevin Ushey. MIT License, text below.

  - Copied from rex: the names of its shortcuts (ls(rex:::.rex$env)), which a package
    that calls rex::register_shortcuts() declares as global variables.


R (https://www.r-project.org), version 4.6.1

  - A list of the names exported by the packages that ship with R (base, stats, utils,
    methods, graphics, grDevices, tools, parallel, compiler, grid, splines, stats4)
    and of its datasets, generated from R's own namespaces, so that undefined-name
    checks know which names exist. It is a list of names only; no R source code is
    included.


air (https://github.com/posit-dev/air)

  - The experimental air-compatible format profile is written to reproduce air's
    output, and is tested against output that air itself produced. No air source code
    or data is included.


